Probably not a good model of reality; the original
ur-paper game versions from which NetHack descends
had a single cell dimensioned at ten feet (English
units, roughly 3 meters) on a side, and the statue
of a PC could tuck into a small corner of that (or a
small corner of a pit in the middle of that).

That does of course beg the question of how the PC
can then move a boulder of sufficient size that it
_does_ fill the hole, but the appropriate viewpoint
is probably that NetHack doesn't slavishly model
reality where that would interfere with playability.

This is, despite tiles upgrades, at heart a game of
characters on a grid where each cell holds one
character, and pushing that model too much toward
realism as to its dimensionalities is a bit of
overkill, when the units are boolean ("pit" or "no
pit") rather than integers (6 foot pit) or floating
point (6.27 foot pit).

